('wikiloc', '200587575', 'https://www.wikiloc.com/hiking-trails/pic-gallinasse-from-coll-de-la-descarga-200587575', 'Pic Gallinasse from Coll de la Descarga', '{"en":"From Coll de la Descarga, rugged loop","ca":"Circular exigent des del Coll de la Descarga","es":"Circular exigente desde Coll de la Descarga"}'::jsonb, 'Pic Gallinasse from Coll de la Descarga Hiking trail in Gausac, Occitanie (France). Download its GPS track and follow the itinerary on a map.  
 
If you''re on mobile, watch the video here: https://youtu.be/5CMGC9rQGTc 
 
This is a hike I never tire of. It has everything I look for in a mountain day—challenging terrain, breathtaking scenery, and a true sense of remoteness. Though it’s a ‘there-and-back’ route, the sheer scale of the landscape means a second look is never enough to take it all in. 
 
This hike delivered a rugged adventure mixed with serene solitude. Highlights included sweeping views of snow-covered peaks and an unexpected encounter with a local paraglider. While snow conditions made progress difficult at times, the day unfolded under clear skies, with light winds and unforgettable moments along the ridges and summits. 
 
Trail Notes 
 
Initial Ascent 
 
The day began with a steady climb through patchy snow, setting off from Coll de la Descarga. The Refugi de Batère (0.7 km) provided an early landmark, nestled in the landscape. The ascent towards the Mines de Batère (1.9 km) revealed remnants of the area’s mining past, an interesting contrast against the surrounding forest and open slopes. 
 
Snow Challenges 
 
Snow became more consistent above 1,700 m, reaching depths of up to 30 cm in places. The surface was soft with hard, frozen layers beneath, slowing progress significantly. Frequent switching between crampons and bare boots was required due to alternating snowfields and exposed rock, making for a frustrating but necessary compromise. 
 
Ridges and Peaks 
 
From Pic de Pel de Ca to Pic Gallinassa, the ridgeline offered spectacular panoramas. Clear blue skies framed the snowy peaks, with the Canigou massif stealing the show from the summit of Pic Gallinassa (2,426 m). 
 
Unexpected Encounter 
 
On Pic Gallinasse, I encountered the only other hiker of the day—a paraglider who had followed my footprints up the snowy ridge. Preparing for his flight home to Céret, he had a GoPro mounted on his helmet to capture the descent. I offered to film his take-off, and within minutes, I was once again alone on the summit, watching him disappear into the sky. 
 
Conditions 
 
Weather: Clear skies, light winds (~5 km/h), temperatures hovering around 3–4°C. 
 
Snow: Patchy coverage, varying from soft to hard-frozen. Crampons were required all day due to frequent ice patches. Normally, I would remove them on snow-free sections, but this would have required at least 50 transitions throughout the day. 
 
 
Practical Tips 
 
Timing: Wikiloc timings can sometimes be a little vague. To give you an idea, in mixed winter conditions, it takes me 3 hours 20 minutes to climb from Coll de la Descarga to the summit of Pic Gallinasse. I like to keep a good pace, but maybe take a 5 minute break every 30 minutes or so, with short photo stops along the way. 
 
Snow Gear: In winter, crampons and an ice axe are essential as some sections of the ridge are steep. Be prepared for frequent transitions between snow and rock. 
 
Route Finding: On the ridge to Pic de Pel de Ca, avoid descending onto the southern side—stay high to keep clear of the boulder field. 
 
Solitude: Expect long stretches without seeing another person. This adds to the experience but requires self-sufficiency if hiking alone. 
 
Wildlife: Isard hoofprints were everywhere, but actual sightings were rare—I spotted only two on this occasion. 
 
Final Thoughts 
 
This hike offered a rewarding mix of challenges and natural beauty. The experience of the paraglider soaring away underscored the region’s adventurous spirit. Despite the snow’s impact on progress, the day was deeply satisfying—one of those hikes that lingers in the memory long after the last footprints have faded from the snow.', '{"en":"This 13 km out-and-back route to Pic de Gallinassa begins at Coll de la Descarga, passing the Refugi de Batère early on. The initial ascent is steady, leading through the historic Mines de Batère before gaining elevation towards the summit.\n\nExpect challenging conditions above 1,700m, where snow can be deep and varied, requiring frequent changes between crampons and bare boots. The route offers a rugged adventure with a sense of remoteness, suitable for experienced hikers prepared for varied terrain and potential snow. Total elevation gain is 1083m.","ca":"Aquesta ruta d''anada i tornada de 13 km al Pic de Gallinassa comença al Coll de la Descarga, passant pel Refugi de Batère al principi. L''ascens inicial és constant, travessant les històriques Mines de Batère abans de guanyar alçada cap al cim.\n\nEspereu condicions desafiadores per sobre dels 1.700m, on la neu pot ser profunda i variada, requerint canvis freqüents entre grampons i botes. La ruta ofereix una aventura robusta amb una sensació de solitud, adequada per a excursionistes experimentats preparats per a terrenys variats i neu potencial. El desnivell acumulat és de 1083m.","es":"Esta ruta de ida y vuelta de 13 km al Pic de Gallinassa comienza en el Coll de la Descarga, pasando por el Refugio de Batère al principio. El ascenso inicial es constante, atravesando las históricas Minas de Batère antes de ganar altura hacia la cumbre.\n\nEspere condiciones desafiantes por encima de los 1.700m, donde la nieve puede ser profunda y variada, requiriendo cambios frecuentes entre crampones y botas. La ruta ofrece una aventura robusta con una sensación de soledad, adecuada para excu